**Files:** `vibrantix-client-panel/src/hooks/useTasks.js:7-13`

**Root Cause:** `staleTime: 60_000` means a cached result is served for 60 seconds without a network request. A task assigned to the user in another session will not appear for up to 60 seconds. The Refresh button exists but there is no visual indicator that data may be stale (e.g., a "last refreshed X seconds ago" label).

**Claude Fix Prompt:**
Consider reducing `staleTime` to 30_000ms for the tasks query since task assignments are time-sensitive. Optionally add a "Last refreshed X minutes ago" text near the Refresh button using the `dataUpdatedAt` value from `useQuery`:
```jsx
const { data, dataUpdatedAt, isFetching, refetch } = useTasks(filters);
// In the header:
const lastUpdated = dataUpdatedAt ? formatDistanceToNow(new Date(dataUpdatedAt), { addSuffix: true }) : null;
```

**Files:** `vibrantix-client-panel/src/app/(private)/tasks/page.jsx:132,158`

**Root Cause:** Both `DueDateCell` and `AssignedCell` use `try { ... } catch { return null; }`. A `null` return from a React function component renders an empty cell — no "—" fallback, no visual indicator that the date was unparseable. Any task with a malformed `dueDate` or `assignedAt` ISO string will silently show a blank cell.

**Claude Fix Prompt:**
Replace `return null` with a fallback in both cells:
```jsx
// DueDateCell:
try { formatted = format(new Date(task.dueDate), 'MMM d, yyyy'); }
catch { return <Text fontSize="xs" color={subColor}>—</Text>; }

// AssignedCell:
try { rel = formatDistanceToNow(new Date(assignedAt), { addSuffix: true }); }
catch { return <Text fontSize="xs" color={subColor}>—</Text>; }
```

**Files:** `vibrantix-backend/src/modules/tasks/tasks.normalise.js:281,328`

**Root Cause:** `id: \`${review._id}_${item.itemId}\`` — if `item.itemId` is `undefined` or `null`, all action items without an `itemId` within the same `ManagementReview` document will share the id `"<reviewId>_undefined"`. Only the last one survives in any dedup-by-id logic. Same for ContinuousImprovement: `id: \`${ci._id}_${initiative.initId}\``. React table rows using `rowKey="id"` will generate duplicate key warnings and potentially render wrong data.

**Claude Fix Prompt:**
Use a stable fallback: the array index is not stable across fetches, but the item's own MongoDB `_id` (if it has one) or a hash of its content is. Best approach — use the item's index in the array as a tie-breaker:
```js
// In normaliseActionItem, pass index from the flat-map:
// Caller: .map((item, idx) => normaliseActionItem(review, item, idx))
function normaliseActionItem(review, item, idx = 0) {
  return {
    id: item._id ? item._id.toString() : `${review._id}_${item.itemId ?? idx}`,
    ...
  };
}
// Same pattern for normaliseCIInitiative
```
